 # process-email
 
-One pass takes the single oldest message in your inbox, does whatever
-it asks, and answers it on the list. The answer is always a reply; any
-patches go out first as their own series, which the reply cites. Loop
-the skill to drain the inbox.
+One pass takes the single inbox message its argument names, does
+whatever it asks, and answers it on the list. The answer is always a
+reply; any patches go out first as their own series, which the reply
+cites.
 
 The list is a maildir carried by jj on the `mail` bookmark.
 zib/patch-review-workflow.org describes the whole workflow; this skill
@@ -91,14 +92,19 @@ A bookmark with no pending mail, missing or merged in full, is
 pointed at the list's tip first. It has nothing to rebase, so the
 rebase alone would leave it behind on an old list.
 
-## 2. Pick the oldest inbox message
+## 2. Take the message
 
-    id="$(notmuch search --sort=oldest-first --output=messages \
-        --limit=1 tag:inbox)"
+The skill's argument names the message to answer, as a notmuch id,
+`id:<message-id>`. It is required; you do not choose the message:
+
+    id="$ARGUMENTS"
     thread="$(notmuch search --output=threads "${id}")"
     msgid="${id#id:}"      # the bare Message-ID, for headers
 
-If there is none, the inbox is empty: stop without writing anything.
+If there is no argument, stop without writing anything. If the named
+message is not tagged `inbox` (`notmuch count "${id} and tag:inbox"`
+prints 0), it is already answered or archived: stop without writing
+anything.
 
 Every inbox message gets an answer, even if it is a short
 acknowledgement. Replying is the only way a message leaves the inbox,
@@ -274,11 +280,6 @@ Check the outcome from outside, not from what you printed:
 If the message is not `replied`, the reply's In-Reply-To is wrong. If
 the commits differ, the push failed. Fix it before stopping.
 
